  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 144 AM CDT Fri Apr 17 2026\n\nSouth winds will increase today ahead of an approaching cold\nfront. This will result in temperatures climbing into the middle\nto upper 80s this afternoon. These values will flirt with record\nhighs, especially across the southern half of our region. Tying\nor breaking any records will be very dependent on if convection\ndevelops. Models indicate at least some widely scattered \nconvection is possible this afternoon. Confidence is not very \nhigh in how much coverage we will see, but 20-30% chances seem \nwarranted for now between 17z-00z.\n\nBy 06z tonight, models show the cold front extending through \ncentral Illinois into southwest Missouri. Showers and \nthunderstorms will begin spreading into the PAH forecast area from\nthe northwest late this evening, spreading across the rest of \nthe area by mid morning Saturday. Peak chances of 80-100% will \nbe across southern Illinois and southeast Missouri from 08z-18z,\nand across southwest Indiana and west Kentucky from 12z-21z. \nThe potential for thunderstorms will be mainly along and ahead \nof the cold front. With the cold front moving through during the\nmorning and early afternoon, thunderstorm chances will decrease\nfrom west to east from late morning through the afternoon. \nShowers will linger behind the front ahead of the associated \nupper trof, so showers will then gradually taper off late \nSaturday afternoon through Saturday evening. Forecast rainfall \ntotals have trended upward a little, with QPF now from near a \nhalf an inch to around one inch.\n\nSPC Day 1 Convective Outlook includes northwest/west portions of\nthe PAH forecast area in a Marginal to Slight Risk of severe\nthunderstorms, mainly northwest of a line from Mount Carmel to\nMarion Illinois, to Cape Girardeau and Poplar Bluff Missouri.\nThis potential will be mainly late this evening to a little\nafter midnight with the initial onset of thunderstorms moving \ninto our region. The main hazards with storms will be damaging \nwind gusts and an isolated tornado potential. \n\nWe will see a significant cool down behind the front through the\nweekend. High temperatures Saturday will be 12 to 20 degrees\ncooler than today. Lows Saturday night will be 5 to 10 degrees \nbelow normal in the upper 30s to lower 40s. Highs Sunday will \nbe seasonable in the 60s to around 70 degrees. Winds will shift\nto the south late Sunday night into Monday, beginning an \nwarming trend. Temperatures by mid to late week will be around \n10 degrees above normal.\n\nHigh pressure will keep conditions dry through the first half of\nthe work week. Models differ quite a bit in timing of a late\nweek system. The latest model blend produces 20% chances of\nshowers and thunderstorms Thursday for now.",
